course: dictionary
lesson: 4663
title: A hoe in the garden
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: You can work out what a word means from the words around it. This is called its context. The poem says «to take a large hoe and a shovel also». A hoe is listed next to a shovel, and you dig with a shovel, so a hoe must be a tool for the garden too.
explain: Read on for more clues. The poem goes on: «to dig till you gently perspire». Digging with a hoe is hard work, and it makes you warm and sweaty. So the poem is describing a gardening job that uses a hoe.
explain: Hoe can be a thing or an action. As a noun, a hoe is the tool itself. As a verb, to hoe means to dig, weed or loosen soil. To check your meaning, swap it into the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
fact: A hoe is a garden tool with a long handle and a flat blade.
fact: The blade of a hoe is used to cut weeds and loosen the soil.
fact: To hoe means to dig, weed or loosen soil with a hoe.
fact: Farmers in history hoed their fields by hand, and a hoe helps keep weeds down so vegetables can grow.
fact: The verb changes with time: «hoed» is the past, «hoeing» is happening now.
exercise: What is a hoe? | *A garden tool with a long handle and a flat blade | A cup for drinking tea | A small bird that lives on farms
exercise: Mia hoed the weeds in the school vegetable patch. What does hoed mean here? | *Dug or weeded the soil with a hoe | Sang to the flowers | Ate the vegetables | Painted the fence
exercise: Why might a gardener hoe a vegetable patch? | *To keep weeds down so the vegetables can grow | To scare away the birds | To make the soil wet and sticky | To make the bed longer
test: In a story, a gardener leans on his hoe. What is a hoe here? | A stick for walking up a hill | A kind of hat worn in the sun | *A garden tool with a flat blade on a long handle
test: In «The hoe is in the shed», what kind of word is hoe? | A question word | *A noun, a thing | An adjective, a describing word | A verb, an action
exam: Which sentence uses hoe as a verb? | The farmer bought a new hoe | The hoe leaned against the shed | A hoe has a long handle | *Tomorrow we will hoe the bean rows
